# TAP iOS SDK

When the user wants to build an iOS app with a Tap device, read the `tapkit-getting-started` skill first. Look in `.cursor/skills/tapkit-getting-started/SKILL.md`, then `plugins/tapkit-ios-sdk/skills/tapkit-getting-started/SKILL.md`.

- Order: add the framework and `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`, pair the Tap in Settings → Bluetooth, `addDelegate`, `setDefaultTAPInputMode` (and `setDefaultTAPXRState` for TapXR and TapBand), then `start()`. `start()` alone delivers no taps.
- The SDK connects only to TAP devices already paired in iOS Settings. It does not scan or show a pairing UI.
- One singleton, `TAPKit.sharedKit`. Delegate methods are optional and run on the main queue. `forIdentifiers: nil` targets every connected device.
- TapXR (current developer firmware) and TapBand share the same BLE API and the full gesture set (taps, thumb swipes, pinches with hold, fist with hold). TapBand is coming soon: https://www.tapwithus.com/tapband-waitlist/ — never call it unsupported or a missing target.
- There is no Swift Package or CocoaPod. Build `TAPKit.framework` and embed it (Embed & Sign).
- `TAPInputMode.text()` types on the system keyboard and does not call `tapped` or `moused`. Use `.controller()` to receive taps in the app.
- The sensor sends raw class indexes (100–114). iOS TAPKit post-processes them into `TAPXRAirGesture` edges on `tapXRAirGestured` (click, drag, drop, swipe, fist begin/end, with debounce and hold latching). Knob and D-Pad recipes (`tapkit-knob`, `tapkit-dpad`) use those edges. Do not switch on 100–114 in `tapXRAirGestured`.
- `setIMUSensitivity` takes `TAPIMUGyroSensitivity` and `TAPIMUAcclSensitivity`, not raw integers.
- Do not invent APIs, Bluetooth UUIDs, or enum values. Test with a real device. There is no simulator.
